     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><u>Hint</u> : don&#39;t forget where you are at :)</div><div><br></div><div><u>Explanation :</u></div><div>Classical timezone issue.</div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(46,46,46);font-family:arial;font-size:12.8000001907349px\">&quot;2015-04-14 IDT&quot; -&gt; the IDT means </span><span style=\"color:rgb(135,135,135);font-size:medium;line-height:19.2000007629395px\">Israeli Daylight Time</span><br></div>When you are using POSIXct it also as a notion of hours , and when you give it a date the default for hours is : 0 (meaning midnight)<div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">&quot;as.Date&quot; function also has a timezone parameter which by default is set to UTC (which is 3 hours before IDT as of today).</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">therefore, the UTC date is actually a day before :) </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><u>Fix :</u></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">keep it in IDT (if that&#39;s what you actually want) :</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">as.Date(x,tz=&quot;Asia/Jerusalem&quot;)<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">OR</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">when you set the POSIXct date in the first place, put it as UTC :</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\">x&lt;-as.POSIXct(&quot;2015-04-14&quot;,tz=&quot;UTC&quot;)</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">(that way as.Date will work as expected)</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br clear=\"all\"><div><div class=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Regards,<div>Daniel Marcous,</div><div>Google, Waze, BI, <i>Data Wizard</i>.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Thu, Apr 16, 2015 at 9:45 AM,  <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">israel-r-...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div s